            <ArticleTitle>Cement fragment from hip arthroplasty causing deep femoral artery pseudoaneurysm.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>633-634</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0218492317732251</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An 81-year-old woman who had undergone total hip arthroplasty 12 years earlier presented with a painful spontaneous hematoma in the proximal left thigh. A left hip radiograph showed a displaced cement fragment from the acetabular component of the hip prosthesis. Computed tomography confirmed an extruded cement fragment causing a large pseudoaneurysm of the deep femoral artery. The patient underwent successful percutaneous embolization of the pseudoaneurysm with coils.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
